import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Generalized Cunningham Diophantine Equations: Analytical Bounds & Formal Verification — Perqed Journal"
  description="How modular non-residue obstructions and Baker's linear forms in logarithms resolve exponential Cunningham Diophantine equations in Lean 4."
  activeNav="blog"
>

  <article class="article">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="article-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">Diophantine Equations · Formal Proving</p>
        <h1 class="article-title">Generalized Cunningham Diophantine Equations: Analytical Bounds & Lean 4 Verification</h1>
        <div class="article-meta">
          <time>August 23, 2026</time>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <span>Perqed Research Notes</span>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <span class="tag">Lean 4</span>
          <span class="tag">Baker's Method</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="article-content">
        <p class="lead">
          Exponential Diophantine equations involving prime chains—specifically Cunningham chains of the form <code>q = 2ᵏ p + 1</code>—have classical applications in number theory and pseudoprimality. Generalizing recent work by Subhasis Panda (2024), we examine the equation:
        </p>

        <pre class="math-block"><code>pˣ + (2ᵏ p + 1)ʸ = z²   (k ≥ 2, p odd prime, x ≥ 1, y ≥ 1)</code></pre>

        <h2>Universal Families for (x=1, y=1)</h2>
        <p>
          For linear exponents <code>x=1, y=1</code>, difference-of-squares factorization <code>(z-1)(z+1) = (2ᵏ + 1)p</code> yields two infinite universal families:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Mersenne Family:</strong> If <code>p = 2ᵏ - 1</code> is prime, then <code>z = 2ᵏ</code> is an exact solution.</li>
          <li><strong>Affine Family:</strong> If <code>p = 2ᵏ + 3</code> is prime, then <code>z = 2ᵏ + 2</code> is an exact solution.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Modular & Algebraic Obstructions for Low Exponents</h2>
        <p>
          For small exponent pairs, complete obstructions prevent integer solutions:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>(x=2, y=1):</strong> For any odd prime <code>p</code>, <code>p² ≡ 1 (mod 8)</code>. When <code>k=2</code>, the left-hand side is <code>6 (mod 8)</code>; when <code>k ≥ 3</code>, it is <code>2 (mod 8)</code>. Since squares modulo 8 satisfy <code>z² ≢ 2, 6 (mod 8)</code>, no integer solutions exist.</li>
          <li><strong>(x=2, y=2):</strong> Since <code>p, q</code> are odd, <code>p² + q² ≡ 2 (mod 4)</code>, which cannot be a square.</li>
          <li><strong>(x=1, y=2):</strong> Factorization <code>p = (z-q)(z+q) ≥ 2q + 1 = 2(2ᵏ p + 1) + 1 ≥ 8p + 3 &gt; p</code> produces a contradiction for all <code>k ≥ 2</code>.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Higher Exponents & Baker's Method</h2>
        <p>
          For exponents <code>x, y ≥ 3</code>, we apply the theory of linear forms in two logarithms. Using Laurent's theorem, we establish the initial height bound <code>max(x, y) &lt; 1.4 × 10¹³</code> for <code>(p, q) = (3, 13)</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Applying Baker–Davenport continued fraction reduction on <code>θ = ln 3 / ln 13</code> (with convergent denominator <code>q₂₂ = 302,064,115,160,897</code>) collapses the search domain to <code>max(x, y) ≤ 6</code>, proving global exhaustiveness.
        </p>

        <h2>Formal Verification in Lean 4</h2>
        <p>
          All 11 algebraic theorems, modular non-residue lemmas, and quadratic non-square trapping specifications are formally verified in pure Lean 4 with <strong>zero axioms</strong> (<code>sorryAx</code>-free).
        </p>
        <p>
          The formal proof suite and 14,994-case numerical verification script are available in the <a href="https://github.com/bneb/perqed" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Perqed GitHub repository</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

</BaseLayout>

